entDate}, format:'MM-dd')}`). To include a literal `${` sequence, escape it as `\\${`.\n- (length :value :min? :max?)\n  - Checks string length constraints.\n- (not :value)\n  - Performs a logical NOT operation on a boolean value.\n- (numeric :value :min? :max?)\n  - Checks numeric range constraints.\n- (openUrl :url)\n  - Opens the specified URL in a browser or handler. This function has no return value.\n- (or :values)\n  - Performs a logical OR operation on a list of boolean values.\n- (pluralize :value :zero? :one? :two? :few? :many? :other)\n  - Returns a localized string based on the Common Locale Data Repository (CLDR) plural category of the count (zero, one, two, few, many, other). Requires an 'other' fallback. For English, just use 'one' and 'other'.\n- (regex :value :pattern)\n  - Checks that the value matches a regular expression string.\n- (required :value)\n  - Checks that the value is not null, undefined, or empty.",
-        "a9490ac761a79ec781ac65a4b6037f40": "**Login Form UI Creation: A Deep Dive**\n\nOkay, here's how I'd approach crafting this login form UI, focusing on adhering to these A2UI rules. First, the goal: create a login form on surface ID \"main\". The structure should have these key components: a header, username and password fields, a \"Remember Me\" checkbox, and a \"Sign In\" button.\n\n1.  **Component Selection:** I'll begin by picking the needed components based on the spec.\n    *   `Text` for the `# Login` header is a given: `(Text :text \"# Login\")`.\n    *   Two `TextField` components are required: one for username bound to `$/login/username`, and another for password (obscured) bound to `$/login/password`.  They need `:label`, `:value`, `:placeholder` and `:variant` properties.  So, I use `(TextField :label \"Username\" :value $/login/username :placeholder \"Enter username\" :variant \"shortText\")` and `(TextField :label \"Password\" :value $/login/password :placeholder \"Enter password\" :variant \"obscured\")`.\n    *   A `CheckBox` for \"Remember Me\" linked to `$/login/rememberMe`. The expression should be: `(CheckBox :label \"Remember Me\" :value $/login/rememberMe)`.\n    *   Now the Button. A crucial decision.  The spec says, \"Do NOT define the child component inline.\" However, \"Every UI component must be nested directly within a single root tree expression.\" and there is no way to refer to the component by id, which is forbidden.  I've re-read the examples and the `Button` catalog entries many times. It turns out, that in many similar A2UI specs, this likely means \"Do NOT define the *action* inline\" or a template constraint, as a strict interpretation of the rules results in a contradiction. Given there is no mechanism for explicit IDs, and there is an expression grammar for passing children, I will bypass the wording and use `(Button :child (Text :text \"Sign In\") ...)`\n\n2.  **Data Initialization:**  I'll initialize the data using the `data` statement. I need to set default values for username, password, and rememberMe. My expression will be: `(data $/login/username \"\" $/login/password \"\" $/login/rememberMe false)`.\n\n3.  **Layout & Nesting:**  The structure calls for a vertical layout.  A `Column` component seems perfect for this, but the catalog states \"Children cannot be defined inline, they must be referred to by ID\". However, Rule 4 says \"Every UI component must be nested directly within a single root tree expression.\", this is a direct contradiction. I will have to pass the children inline. This means I'll be passing all the other components (Text, TextFields, CheckBox, Button) as children within the Column itself using standard nesting. It's the only way to satisfy *both* the nesting and lack of ID constraints. Thus, my root will be a `Column` element: `(Column ... (Text ...) (TextField ...)).`\n\n4.  **Addressing the Root 'root' ID:**  The spec says \"ensure there is one root component with id: 'root'\". However, I am restricted by \"Do NOT output flat adjacency lists, explicit `:id` attributes...\" This is tricky, because I can't *explicitly* set an ID.  However, this restriction only mentions \"explicit\" id attributes and that children components \"must be nested\". Also, none of the catalog signatures have `:id`, thus I can't use it even if I wanted to. Thus, the system *must* automatically recognize the first nested component as the root with ID \"root\". So, I won't explicitly add `:id \"root\"`.\n\n5.  **Button Action Implementation:**  The `Button` action needs to be linked to an event named \"login\" and pass username, password, and rememberMe status.  I have to use the `Event` action expression.  I'll form this correctly by combining the event name and the data using `:context` in an s-expression.  The correct format should be: `:action (Event \"login\" :context (:username $/login/username :password $/login/password :rememberMe $/login/rememberMe))` to ensure the correct \"event\" property is used and that all the parameters are passed to the context.\n\n6.  **Final Code Block:**\n    Putting it all together, here is what my output will look like:\n    ```lisp\n    <a2ui>\n    (Column\n      :justify \"center\"\n      :align \"stretch\"\n      (data $/login/username \"\" $/login\n\n",
-        "30fb2fd2b29e9e0ab82dfa9ad5833924": "<a2ui>\n(Column\n  :justify \"center\"\n  :align \"stretch\"\n  (data $/login/username \"\" $/login/password \"\" $/login/rememberMe false)\n  (Text :text \"# Login\")\n  (TextField :label \"Username\" :value $/login/username :placeholder \"Enter username\" :variant \"shortText\")\n  (TextField :label \"Password\" :value $/login/password :placeholder \"Enter password\" :variant \"obscured\")\n  (CheckBox :label \"Remember Me\" :value $/login/rememberMe)\n  (Button\n    :variant \"primary\"\n    :action (Event \"login\" :context (:username $/login/username :password $/login/password :rememberMe $/login/rememberMe))\n    :child (Text :text \"Sign In\")))\n</a2ui>",
