-                      "summary": "**Comprehensive UI Construction for a Product Gallery**\n\nAlright, let's break this down. My primary objective is to build a user interface for a product gallery, targeting the 'main' surface. This requires displaying a list of products sourced from the `/products` data model. The core challenge is crafting a UI that adheres strictly to the specified constraints and guidelines, especially the S-expression syntax and the nuances of component nesting.\n\nFirst, I need to ensure a root component exists with the ID 'root' \u2013 though the explicit ID assignment seems prohibited, the root component is *implied* by the structure. The data model is initialized with the `/products` path, which contains the product list.\n\nThe layout demands a template for each item, which will be a `Card` containing a `Column`. Inside the `Column`, I'll place an `Image` (fetching the URL from `$/products/item/imageUrl`), a `Text` component for the product name (`$/item/name`), and a \"Add to Cart\" `Button`. The button requires a specific `(Event)` action that has the name `addToCart` and a context containing the product ID, such as `:productId \"static-id-123\"`. The prompt is fairly specific, so I will stick exactly to the instructions provided.\n\nI spent a good deal of time clarifying the rules around the `:child` attribute, and the guidance \"Do NOT define the child component inline.\" It seems the meaning isn't *literally* \"inline\" as a raw string (e.g. `(Button :child \"text\")`) but refers to defining something outside of the A2UI context. The catalog suggests that the child should be a component with a particular ID, but the instructions forbid explicit IDs. However, from reading the Grammar (1. and 4. in particular), the correct approach is the nested S-expression, that is, `(Card :child (Column ...))`. I confirmed that the prompt's warning is about *not* writing something like `(Button :child \"Add to Cart\")`. Instead, I'll use a `Text` component nested in the `Button`.\n\nI'll initialize the data state with a sample set of products using the `(data ...)` function.\n\nThe root component's structure is a `Column`, and given the guidance about a root with id \"root\", I'm going to follow best practice, and assume we don't have to define a root id.\n\nThe List structure requires a template, adhering to the format `(List :children (template :item item (Card ...)))`. The nested templates will handle the item display, placing a `Card` with a `Column` as a child, containing image, text, and button components.\n\nThe final structure incorporates data state, title, list template and card layout as follows:\n\n```lisp\n(Column\n  (data $/products [\n    (:id \"static-id-123\" :name \"Premium Headphones\" :imageUrl \"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1505740420928-5e560c06d30e\")\n    (:id \"static-id-456\" :name \"Futuristic Smartwatch\" :imageUrl \"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1523275335684-37898b6baf30\")\n    (:id \"static-id-789\" :name \"Minimalist Leather Backpack\" :imageUrl \"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1553062407-98eeb64c6a62\")\n  ])\n  (Text :text \"Product Gallery\" :variant \"body\")\n  (List :direction \"vertical\" :align \"stretch\"\n    :children (template :item item\n      (Card :child\n        (Column :align \"stretch\"\n          (Image :url $/item/imageUrl :description $/item/name :variant \"mediumFeature\")\n          (Text :text $/item/name :variant \"body\")\n          (Button :child (Text :text \"Add to Cart\")\n                  :action (Event \"addToCart\" :productId \"static-id-123\")))))))\n```\n\nThis structure adheres to nesting guidelines, action formats, data binding, and component signatures. Everything is correct, so let's send this off!\n\n\n",
