+        "adb904e1d3fb0437e7a005c094a7cd51": "**My Thinking Process for Building the Product Gallery**\n\nOkay, here's how I'm approaching this: I need to generate an A2UI structure for a product gallery. The key is to populate a data model at the `/products` path and build the UI to display the products.  I'll start by initializing the surface 'main' with a root component.  The prompt specifies that the `/products` path should contain a map (or object) of products, where each product has an `id`, `name`, and `imageUrl`. I've got a sample structure for that.\n\nFirst, I will initialize the data using the `(data ...)` function as required, populating a map at `/products` with two products. I will use the product example from the prompt to guide my initial data.\n\nSince this seems to be about building a UI with at least two products, I need to think about how to display them. I know I can't define child components inline based on the instructions. The catalog of signatures is very important: `List`, `Row`, `Column`, `Card`, `Text`, etc. I have to create this UI in a single root expression.  I think I can use a `Column` as the root, then nest `Row`s to arrange the products.\n\nThe trick is, can I build a `List` component directly from the map structure, or do I need to process each product individually? If the product list is statically rendered to exactly two products, I'll go with a static representation, since the data model explicitly states it requires a map. The prompt wants a map, so I will ensure that is the data structure.\n\nI have to carefully adhere to the A2UI rules, particularly the strict nesting requirement: \"Every UI component must be nested directly within a single root tree expression.\"  I can't use adjacency lists or IDs to refer to children outside their parent. The other tricky part is the `:child` parameter of some components like `Card`, which seems to accept an ID. That is confusing. I must stick to nesting, and if a component like `Card` does not allow nesting, I will just avoid it.  The examples and the grammar show that I can nest components positionally.\n\nHere's my plan:\n1.  Initialize data for `/products` as a map using the `(data ...)` function.\n2.  Build a root `Column` component.\n3.  Nest the data initializer within the `Column`.\n4.  Add a `Text` header to the `Column`.\n5.  Add a `Row` to the `Column`.\n6.  Inside the `Row`, add two nested `Column`s, one for each product.\n7.  In each inner `Column`, add an `Image` and a `Text` component, using data bindings from the `/products` path.\n8.  I also have to check the catalog signature for each component: only use properties that exist in the catalog.\n\nI have to carefully double-check everything:  the placement of the sentinel tags,  the nesting rules (no flat lists, no IDs), and the data model structure.\n\nI also have to check whether the root component requires an `:id \"root\"` attribute or not. The instructions say it should have `id: 'root'`, but then the general rules say not to have any IDs at all! It seems like the rules are a little self-contradictory. I interpret the instructions as meaning I should not output an ID.  I can't put that `:id` in there, because I'm not allowed to output explicit `:id` attributes according to the rules.\n\nTherefore, I'll build a neat, nested UI structure, following the catalog signatures, nesting rules, and required format, and then I'll add the sentinel tags. This will strictly adhere to every instruction provided in the prompt.\n\n\n",
