+                      "summary": "**UI Construction Process: A Deep Dive**\n\nOkay, here's the breakdown of my thought process in building this User Settings UI. First, I locked onto the core goal: a UI on the 'main' surface with a `Tabs` component for Profile and Notifications, a `Modal` triggered by a \"Delete Account\" button, and confirmation dialog. My immediate concern was sticking to the rules. No raw JSON, required tags, and especially, the crucial \"no inline children\" rule. This got me digging through the component catalog to understand how children are defined via their `id` or strictly nested in this S-expression format.\n\nThe catalog check confirmed the nesting approach. Now, it was about architecting the hierarchy. I envisioned a root `Column` as the foundation. I'm adding `data` expressions inside the `Column` to initialize user data. Then comes the header, the `Tabs` with the Profile tab (a `TextField`) and the Notifications tab (a `CheckBox`), a horizontal `Divider`, and finally the `Modal`. The `Modal` itself has the trigger button and the confirmation content, complete with its own two buttons in a `Row` component.\n\nThe next challenge was translating these ideas into the S-expression syntax. I started with defining `Tabs :tabs`, which I quickly realized needed an array of objects. Rule 2, and the examples, helped me land on the array representation. The Notification tab label was a little confusing with the `#`, so I defaulted to including it.\n\nThe modal construction required reviewing the signatures for `Modal`, `Button`, and `Column`. I added event handling with actions on the `Button` components like opening, closing, and confirming the deletion. I made sure to use `(Text :text \"...\")` as the children of the buttons as instructed. I was meticulous with each component, ensuring everything was present and accounted for: data initialization for fields and checkboxes, nesting requirements for children in `Column` and `Row` components.\n\nI then revisited the `id` rule for the root component. While the catalog doesn't list `:id` on `Column`, I'm adding `:id \"root\"` to the outermost `Column`, as the instructions require it.\n\nI addressed the absence of surfaceId 'main' by assuming it's implied by our environment, given there are no rules to define it. I focused on defining the root component directly.\n\nFinally, I reviewed every detail \u2013 the component signatures, the allowed properties, the mandatory actions \u2013 to build what I believe is a clean, compliant S-expression that achieves the desired UI. I'm confident that this is a solid output.\n\n\n",
