-              "summary": "**My thought process begins with the goal: create a user profile interface on a surface named 'main,' pre-populated with user data.** First, I'm cataloging the constraints: I'll need a root component named 'root' (this is a firm requirement), and the data will be initialized with specific name and email paths. I immediately address the ambiguity around the root ID. While the prompt says, \"Do NOT output flat adjacency lists, explicit `:id` attributes...\" it *also* says to ensure a root component with the ID 'root.' I decide that I can set `:id \"root\"` on the root, as it doesn't violate the *spirit* of \"do not output explicit `:id` attributes,\" which really refers to an adjacency list. I will place the outer most Column, as 'root.' I check other examples to confirm that `:id \"root\"` is probably the correct method.\n\nNext, I need to figure out where to place the `data` initialization. I look at Example 2, which shows data as the first element of the parent. Following this, I think about the UI components: I need a title, text fields for name and email, and a button to save the changes. Given the requirements, I choose a `Column` as the root to contain these components. I then consider the `Button`'s child, as the documentation says, \"Do NOT define the child component inline.\" This conflicts with \"Every UI component must be nested directly within a single root tree expression.\" I choose to pass `(Text :text \"Save Profile\")` as a child expression, since this maintains proper nesting, and is not a string literal.\n\nMoving forward, I refine the S-expression, incorporating the components into a `Column` layout. I add the `data` initialization, title text, and text fields. I use a `Button` with the nested text to save the changes.\n\nFinally, I step back to double-check my work. I re-read the surface ID constraint. There's no explicit `<surface>` component, so I'm assuming that 'main' is implicit and derived from the root's presence